<Table class="oracle.dbtools.crest.model.design.relational.Table" directorySegmentName="seg_0" id="6261D7D5-B7D0-6A08-A99D-9DD6652D863D" name="RELATIONS">
<createdBy>stb</createdBy>
<createdTime>2020-02-04 14:57:52 UTC</createdTime>
<comment><![CDATA[Relationship connecting 2 entities (could be the same one)

Types are 1:1, M:1 (functional), M:N, ISAR or ISAS
RELA_TYPE is partially redundant with MAPTYPE_FROM_TO/_TO_FROM (see constraints)

ISAS is a 1:1 representation representing a (exclusive) subtype 
"a Partner is either a Person or a Company")

ISAR is a role-subtype, an entity can take up several roles ("a Customer can be none, one or all of a Tenant, an Owner, a Dealer, an OEM-customer")

Conditions for ISAS/R
    both:  	The SubEntities have a mandatory relationship with the SuperEntity (meaning the cannot exists without the SuperEntity)
    ISAS:	SuperEntity has 1:1 mandatory relationships to more than 1 SubEntity. The relationships must be in a arc (meaning they are  exlusive).
    ISAR:	SuperEntity has 1:1 optinal relationships with at least one SubEntity. 

ASSOC_FROM_TO / ASSOC_TO_FROM
	can be empty for type ISAS/R but are mandatory for all other types

ARC_ID_FROM / ARC_ID_TO
	An end of a relationship is in an ARC (meaning exclusiv in respect to all others relationships in the same ARC)
	
MAPTYPE_FROM_TO, MANDATORY_FROM_TO (likewise for ...TO_FROM)
indicate the cardinality and optionality in one direction.
MAPTYPE	 MANDATORY   Cardinality
    1	 TRUE	      	 exactly 1
    1	 FALSE		 0 - 1
    M	 TRUE		 1 - M
    M	 FALSE		 0 - M

HIST_FROM_TO / HIST_TO_FROM
	TRUE	Relationship is historized (temporal) in this direction, meaning it can change over time.
	Does only make sense for M:1 (function), 1:1 or ISAS/R relationships. It allows to model a "1" relationship even if it can change over time.
	(This type has to be implemented in a data model with an intersection table, containing a valid-from/valid-to mechanism

Subtype of MODELELEMENT. RELA_ID is always identical to the corresponding MODE_ID]]></comment>
